1: # Actual native Cox mutations and two complete source-loop fan families
2: 
3: This proof concerns the positive selected-cusp source loop of K55, with
4: its full source, Cartier line, physical height, scalar and finite-cap
5: data. All products below are the actual ordinary products of K55.
6: The preceding diagnostic is retained separately. Its finite checks
7: verify the written maps; the induction below proves the stated
8: families for every nonnegative integer.
9: 
10: ## CF.1. Three exact lattice and native-star arrows
11: 
12: Write the ordered fan basis as \(P=(v\ w)\). Its triangles are
13: \((0,v,w)\) and \((v,w,v+w)\), with their lattice translates.
14: In the first triangle use \((a,b,c)\), with
15: \(q=abc, Z=b,V=c\). The three curves have native first charts
16: \[
17:  C_0:(z,n_0,n_1)=(b,a,c),\qquad
18:  C_1:(z,n_0,n_1)=(c,a,b),\qquad
19:  C_2:(z,n_0,n_1)=(a,b,c).                              \tag{CF.1}
20: \]
21: Their native second charts are exactly GF.11 and satisfy
22: \(z'=z^{-1},n'_r=zn_r\), including every complete period unit.
23: 
24: The three periodic flips have the following actual new bases and
25: new labels for the flopped curve:
26: \[
27:  Q_0=\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\0&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
28:  Q_1=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\-1&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
29:  Q_2=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\0&1\end{pmatrix},
30:  \qquad P'=PQ_i,\qquad i'=(2,2,0)_i.                  \tag{CF.2}
31: \]
32: For \(C_0\), the old quadrilateral is
33: \((0,v,w,w-v)\); its old diagonal is \((0,w)\), and its new
34: diagonal joins \(v\) to \(w-v\). The new two triangles are those
35: of \((v,w-v)\). For \(C_1\), use
36: \((0,w,v,v-w)\), giving the basis \((v-w,w)\).
37: For \(C_2\), the quadrilateral is \((0,v,w,v+w)\), and its
38: new diagonal is \((0,v+w)\). The second triangle of
39: \((v,v+w)\), translated by \(-v\), is \((0,w,v+w)\).
40: These are literal triangle equalities, repeated over the period
41: lattice. Each basis change and its inverse is integral.
42: 
43: The actual torus characters are, respectively,
44: \[
45:  (Z',V')=(ZV,V),\qquad (Z',V')=(Z,ZV),\qquad
46:  (Z',V')=(Z/V,V),\qquad q'=q.                         \tag{CF.3}
47: \]
48: Their new first-triangle coordinates are
49: \[
50:  (a/c,bc,c),\qquad (a/b,b,bc),\qquad (ac,b/c,c).        \tag{CF.4}
51: \]
52: Thus in the native first charts of the flopped curves, (CF.3)
53: is exactly the standard other-ruling arrow
54: \[
55:  (z,n_0,n_1)\longmapsto
56:   (\zeta,u,v)=(n_0/n_1,zn_1,n_1)                     \tag{CF.5}
57: \]
58: for \(i=0,2\). For \(i=1\) the last two coordinates are
59: \((v,u)\). In each case \(\zeta uv=zn_0n_1=q\).
60: The second new chart uses
61: \((\zeta',u',v')=(n_1/n_0,zn_0,n_0)\), with the same
62: constant interchange for \(i=1\). It has
63: \(\zeta'=\zeta^{-1},(u',v')=\zeta(u,v)\).
64: These formulas are the two quotient charts of the rank-one matrix
65: \[
66:  \begin{pmatrix}n_0&n_1\\zn_0&zn_1\end{pmatrix}.       \tag{CF.6}
67: \]
68: They identify the common complement and both native normal bundles
69: by the actual Cox construction. The normal interchange for
70: \(i=1\) is a constant element of \(U(2)\); a path in \(U(2)\)
71: from that element to the identity acts on the whole normal bundle.
72: It introduces no parameter-dependent normal clutch.
73: 
74: Here are the full unit maps. Write the old two periods as GF.2,
75: with unit matrix \(A=(A_{rs})\). The new generators are the
76: columns of \(Q_i\) in that period basis. The actual new units are
77: \[
78:  A'_{rs}=\prod_{h,j}A_{hj}^{(Q_i^{-1})_{rh}(Q_i)_{js}}. \tag{CF.7}
79: \]
80: In the three cases their matrices are
81: \[
82:  A'_0=\begin{pmatrix}
83:  A_{11}A_{21}&A_{12}A_{22}/(A_{11}A_{21})\\
84:  A_{21}&A_{22}/A_{21}
85:  \end{pmatrix},
86: \]
87: \[
88:  A'_1=\begin{pmatrix}
89:  A_{11}/A_{12}&A_{12}\\
90:  A_{11}A_{21}/(A_{12}A_{22})&A_{12}A_{22}
91:  \end{pmatrix},
92: \]
93: \[
94:  A'_2=\begin{pmatrix}
95:  A_{11}/A_{21}&A_{11}A_{12}/(A_{21}A_{22})\\
96:  A_{21}&A_{21}A_{22}
97:  \end{pmatrix}.                                      \tag{CF.8}
98: \]
99: Substitution in the new GF.11 second charts gives the second
100: native chart of (CF.5) exactly. For example in case \(i=2\),
101: the second vertex coordinates before applying GF.11 are
102: \[
103:  (u',v',w')=\bigl(A_{11}b,ab/A_{21},A_{21}c/(A_{11}b)\bigr).
104: \]
105: GF.11 sends these to \((c/b,ab,b)\), precisely the second
106: chart of (CF.5). For \(i=0,1\), the new curve is \(C_2\),
107: and the second native charts are directly
108: \((c/a,ab,a)\) and \((b/a,a,ac)\), respectively.
109: These equalities retain all unit factors; their parameter winding
110: is included in (CF.7). In particular
111: \(B'=Q_i^{-1}BQ_i\), without replacing a unit by its winding.
112: 
113: The characters (CF.3), inverse characters and full period changes
114: give the same original source and line maps on their dense overlap.
115: The physical height is literally \(q\). The retained finite cap
116: and source exterior therefore use their original maps there.
117: Every periodic curve orbit has the same free representative charts
118: as K55, so (CF.5)--(CF.8) descend to its whole periodic flop.
119: The unrestricted four-weight Cox identity of K54 intertwines
120: the physical circle with these maps. Transporting the Hermitian
121: metric and applying the positive radial normalization gives its
122: actual common-link arrow; the normal rescaling of K54 carries
123: the full nonlinear units. This supplies the actual family flop,
124: including its native normal conventions.
125: 
126: ## CF.2. Exact selected-coordinate update
127: 
128: Let the old ordinary relative pair at \(C_i\) be \((m_i,n_i)\),
129: and let its central coordinate be the actual sphere return \(s\).
130: K55's explicit cylinder comparison removes the pure-left
131: \(L^6\), giving the native pair \((m_i-6,n_i)\).
132: The actual common-link arrow (CF.5), in the K23 Hopf coordinates,
133: is \(\tau(h,q_H)=(q_H^{-1}hq_H,q_H)\), with the exterior
134: extension \(T(y,q_H)=(q_H^{-1}yq_H,q_H)\). The complex-normal
135: reset is exactly K54's fixed \(J\). Applying their proved
136: relative maps gives the new native pair and central coordinate
137: \[
138:  (m_i-6+2n_i,-n_i),\qquad s'=s+n_i.                   \tag{CF.9}
139: \]
140: The constant normal interchange in (CF.5) acts by a constant
141: oriented four-plane change. Its lift conjugates the two Spin
142: factors separately, through the displayed \(U(2)\) path, and
143: preserves their degrees. Thus the same formula applies to
144: all three cases of (CF.2).
145: 
146: Add the actual new \(L^6\) comparison on the new native star.
147: Its ordinary pair is \((m_i+2n_i,-n_i)\). In terms of the
148: actual ordinary variation and ambient derivative, put
149: \(a=V_{ii}\), \(b=\delta_i\). K31.7's carried single-star
150: frame map gives
151: \[
152:  a=-(m_i+n_i),\qquad b=2n_i+a.
153: \]
154: Consequently the new selected entries and the actual return obey
155: \[
156:  V'_{i'i'}=a,\qquad \delta'_{i'}=2a-b,\qquad
157:  s'=s+\frac{b-a}{2}\pmod {28}.                       \tag{CF.10}
158: \]
159: This derivation uses only the actual selected native star on
160: each side. It does not use a simultaneous-product matrix reset.
161: 
162: ## CF.3. Recovering the other entries from the actual trace maps
163: 
164: For every target with nonzero tangent weights \(w_0,w_1,w_2\),
165: write \(r_i=1/w_i\). The exact GF trace identities and symmetry
166: have the complete solutions
167: \[
168:  V=\operatorname{diag}(I_0/w_0,I_1/w_1,I_2/w_2)+c\,rr^T,
169:  \qquad \delta_j=(W_j+e)/w_j.                         \tag{CF.11}
170: \]
171: Indeed the displayed diagonal is a particular solution. The
172: two vectors \(w_je_j-w_0e_0\) span the kernel of the functional
173: with coefficients \(r\). A symmetric matrix annihilating that
174: plane has image in its orthogonal line, and is exactly
175: \(c rr^T\). The same argument for the derivative leaves
176: the single constant \(e\). All these statements are rational
177: linear algebra applied to the actual integral identities.
178: 
179: At the selected new curve the already proved entries (CF.10)
180: therefore fix
181: \[
182:  c=w_{i'}^2a-w_{i'}I_{i'},\qquad
183:  e=w_{i'}(2a-b)-W_{i'}.                               \tag{CF.12}
184: \]
185: Equations (CF.11)--(CF.12) give the entire actual new matrix
186: and derivative. Their values are integral because they equal
187: the actual integral invariants uniquely, whose existence is
188: the explicit family construction above.
189: 
190: For completeness a target has at most one zero tangent weight.
191: For the zero index \(r\), the other two weights are \(+1,-1\),
192: by primitivity. The same trace equations give
193: \[
194:  V_{jj}=I_j/w_j,\quad V_{rj}=V_{jr}=-I_r/w_j,
195:  \quad V_{pq}=0\quad(p,q\ne r,\ p\ne q),\qquad
196:  \delta_j=(W_j-W_r)/w_j\quad(j\ne r).                 \tag{CF.13}
197: \]
198: Their two remaining entries are precisely \(V_{rr},\delta_r\).
199: For a flop whose selected target is that fixed curve, (CF.10)
200: supplies both. A target with a different retained fixed curve
201: requires its own actual fixed-star data. This describes the
202: exact rank of the trace map and leaves that specific data open.
203: 
204: ## CF.4. A complete horizontal staircase
205: 
206: For every integer \(j\ge0\), set
207: \[
208:  P_j=\begin{pmatrix}1&-j\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.             \tag{CF.14}
209: \]
210: The actual return and complete ordinary invariants on this fan are
211: \[
212:  s_j=1-3j(j+1)\pmod {28},
213: \]
214: \[
215:  V_j=\begin{pmatrix}
216:  0&6(j+1)&-6\\
217:  6(j+1)&18j(j+1)&-6j\\
218:  -6&-6j&0
219:  \end{pmatrix},\qquad
220:  \delta_j=\begin{pmatrix}
221:  -12(j+1)\\-24j(j+1)-12\\12j
222:  \end{pmatrix}.                                      \tag{CF.15}
223: \]
224: The native pairs on its three curves are
225: \[
226:  (6j,-6(j+1)),\quad
227:  (3j(j+1),-21j(j+1)-6),\quad
228:  (-6j-6,6j).                                         \tag{CF.16}
229: \]
230: 
231: Here is an induction by actual family maps. At \(j=0\), (CF.15)
232: is exactly K51/K52 and the return is F56's class one. GF.10a
233: supplies the explicit based homotopy identifying those initial
234: products with K55's chosen product. The \(C_0\) flip of \(P_j\)
235: is precisely \(P_jQ_0=P_{j+1}\); its selected target is \(C_2\).
236: For \(P_j\) the literal complete windings and trace degrees are
237: \[
238:  B_j=\begin{pmatrix}-6&6j\\0&0\end{pmatrix},\quad
239:  l=(0,-6(j+1),0),\quad w=(j,1,-j-1),
240: \]
241: \[
242:  I=(-6(j+1),12j(j+1),6j),\qquad
243:  W=(6,-12j(j+1)-6,6).                                \tag{CF.17}
244: \]
245: These follow by substituting the actual (CF.3)/(CF.7) maps in GF.
246: The target weights \((j+1,1,-j-2)\) are all nonzero.
247: Equation (CF.10) sets its selected entries to
248: \(V'_{22}=0,\delta'_2=12(j+1)\). The unique solution
249: (CF.11)--(CF.12) is (CF.15) with \(j\) replaced by \(j+1\).
250: Thus the full matrix and derivative have been obtained from the
251: actual target, including their off-diagonal entries.
252: The old right integer is \(-6(j+1)\), so
253: \[
254:  s_{j+1}=s_j-6(j+1).
255: \]
256: Summing this actual consecutive-flop identity gives (CF.15).
257: Finally the native comparison gives
258: \(n_i=(\delta_i-V_{ii})/2\) and
259: \(m_i=-(\delta_i+V_{ii})/2-6\), which proves (CF.16).
260: 
261: ## CF.5. The adjacent complete family
262: 
263: Flop \(C_1\) on each of the just constructed actual families.
264: Its new basis is
265: \[
266:  R_j=P_jQ_1=\begin{pmatrix}j+1&-j\\-1&1\end{pmatrix},
267:  \qquad j\ge0.                                       \tag{CF.18}
268: \]
269: Equation (CF.16) gives its actual native right integer.
270: The same Cox family arrow (CF.5)--(CF.10) therefore gives
271: \[
272:  [\Phi_{R_j}]=1-3j(j+1)-21j(j+1)-6
273:              =-5-24j(j+1)\pmod {28}.                 \tag{CF.19}
274: \]
275: This computes the actual return on every fan (CF.18), including
276: \(j=0\). Its sequence has period seven: modulo 28 it equals
277: \(23+4j(j+1)\), and shifting \(j\) by seven changes this
278: by \(56j+224\). The seven consecutive values are
279: \[
280:                          23,3,19,15,19,3,23 .
281: \]
282: They give the exact range \(\{3,15,19,23\}\); the sequence
283: is not constant, so seven is its least positive period.
284: In particular
285: \(R_1=(\begin{smallmatrix}2&-1\\-1&1\end{smallmatrix})\)
286: has the actual positive return class three.
287: 
288: All original physical source and cap maps are carried through
289: the specified finite sequence for each \(j\). This proof assigns
290: no value to a fan outside its computed domain. The exact algebra
291: and repeated-basis/fan consistency checks are retained under
292: \path{code/part3_root/source_cusp_mutation_diagnostic.*}; their
293: first unsimplified symbolic-matrix equality attempt is preserved.
294: The written Cox/period maps, native-frame comparison and induction
295: are the proof for all \(j\).
